"An event sourcing diagram shows state reconstructed from an authoritative ordered history of domain events.\n\nUse when: Explain event persistence, replay, and materialized views. Review aggregate event ordering and state reconstruction.\n\nAvoid when: Events are merely notifications emitted after ordinary row updates.\n\nVisual grammar: An append-only stream identifies ordered events per entity or aggregate. A fold or apply path derives current state from prior state and events. Separate projections and optional snapshots show derived, rebuildable artifacts.\n\nBuild: Choose one aggregate and its event vocabulary. Draw command validation followed by append and state application. Add replay, projection, and optional snapshot paths. Place authoritative history separately from derived views with unobstructed event labels.\n\nCheck: The authoritative record is identified consistently. Ordering and optimistic-concurrency rules are explicit. Snapshots do not replace the underlying history without a stated policy. Replay and live-update arrows remain distinct from stream contents.\n\nPitfalls: Assuming an event broker is automatically an authoritative event store. Calling captured table changes domain events without considering their meaning.\n\nExample: CartOpened, ItemAdded, and ItemRemoved reconstruct a cart; a separate projection serves the current item list.\n\nAlternatives: CQRS diagram, Change-data-capture diagram."
